## Claim
Text: "Americans advised to reconsider travel to Ghana due to COVID-19."
Claim author: U.S. Embassy in Ghana 
Claim date: September 22, 2020
Claim origin: https://gh.usembassy.gov/ghana-covid-19-information/

## Initial Q&A
### Did the U.S. Embassy in Ghana publish an official travel advisory on or around September 22, 2020, concerning COVID-19?
Answer: Yes, the U.S. Embassy in Ghana did publish an official travel advisory on September 22, 2020, concerning COVID-19. The archived page titled "Ghana Travel Advisory – Level 3 Reconsider Travel" from that date states, "The Centers for Diseace [sic] Control and Prevention (CDC) has issued a Level 3 Travel Health Notice for Ghana due to COVID-19."

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### Did the specific advisory text contain the phrase or instruction for U.S. citizens to "reconsider travel" to Ghana?
Answer: Yes, the specific advisory text explicitly states "Level 3 Reconsider Travel" in its title, directly instructing U.S. citizens to reconsider travel to Ghana.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### Was the primary justification given for this advisory the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Yes, the primary justification given for the advisory was the COVID-19 pandemic. The search result explicitly states that the "Level 3 Travel Health Notice for Ghana" was issued "due to COVID-19," and it directs travelers to consult the Department of State’s and the embassy's COVID-19 pages for more information. While other security concerns like crime and civil unrest are mentioned, they are listed as separate cautions for specific regions, not as the main reason for the overall "Reconsider Travel" advisory level.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### Was this advisory issued by the U.S. Embassy in Accra, Ghana, as opposed to another U.S. government entity like the State Department in Washington?
Answer: The advisory was issued by the U.S. Department of State, not the U.S. Embassy in Accra. The search result explicitly states, "The Department of State has issued the following Travel Advisory for Ghana," which contradicts the claim's attribution to the Embassy.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### Does the archived webpage at the origin URL (https://gh.usembassy.gov/ghana-covid-19-information/) from September 2020 contain the text of this travel advisory?
Answer: Yes, the archived webpage from September 22, 2020, does contain the text of the travel advisory. The page explicitly states, "Ghana Travel Advisory – Level 3 Reconsider Travel," and references a Level 3 Travel Health Notice from the CDC "due to COVID-19," which matches the core claim.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### What was the official advisory level (e.g., Level 3) assigned to Ghana by the U.S. Department of State on September 22, 2020?
Answer: The official advisory level assigned to Ghana by the U.S. Department of State on September 22, 2020, was **Level 3 – Reconsider Travel**.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210122073123/https://gh.usembassy.gov/travel-alert-092220/

### What were the specific COVID-19 metrics or conditions in Ghana cited in the advisory as the reason for the "reconsider travel" recommendation?
Answer: The search result does not provide specific COVID-19 metrics or conditions cited in the U.S. Embassy advisory for the "reconsider travel" recommendation.

